sn11.11:1.9 | S: As long as I live, may I speak the truth. | T: (6) “‘As long as I live may I speak the truth.’
sn11.11:1.10 | S: As long as I live, may I be free of anger, or should anger arise, may I quickly get rid of it. | T: (7) “‘As long as I live may I be free from anger, and if anger should arise in me may I dispel it quickly.’
sn11.11:1.11 | S: In a former life, when Sakka was a human being, he undertook seven vows. And it was because of undertaking these that he achieved the status of Sakka. | T: “In the past, bhikkhus, when Sakka, lord of the devas, was a human being, he adopted and undertook these seven vows by the undertaking of which he achieved the status of Sakka.
sn11.11:2.1 | S: A child who respects their parents, | T: “When a person supports his parents,
sn11.11:2.2 | S: and honors the elders in the family, | T: And respects the family elders;
sn11.11:2.3 | S: whose speech is gentle and courteous, | T: When his speech is gentle and courteous,
sn11.11:2.4 | S: and has given up backbiting; | T: And he refrains from divisive words;
sn11.11:3.1 | S: who’s committed to getting rid of stinginess, | T: When he strives to remove meanness,
sn11.11:3.2 | S: is truthful, and has mastered anger: | T: Is truthful, and vanquishes anger,
sn11.11:3.3 | S: the gods of the thirty-three declare | T: The Tavatiṁsa devas call him
sn11.11:3.4 | S: them to be a true person.” | T: Truly a superior person.”

## sn11.15
sn11.15:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: At Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove.
sn11.15:1.2 | S: And then Sakka, lord of gods, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, stood to one side, and said to him:
sn11.15:1.3 | S: “Sir, what is a delightful place?” | T: “Venerable sir, what is a delightful place?”
sn11.15:2.1 | S: “Shrines in parks and forests, | T: The Blessed One: “Shrines in parks and woodland shrines,
sn11.15:2.2 | S: well-made lotus ponds, | T: Well-constructed lotus ponds:
sn11.15:2.3 | S: are not worth a sixteenth part | T: These are not worth a sixteenth part
sn11.15:2.4 | S: of a delightful human being. | T: Of a delightful human being.
sn11.15:3.1 | S: Whether in village or wilderness, | T: “Whether in a village or forest,
sn11.15:3.2 | S: in a valley or the uplands, | T: In a valley or on the plain—
sn11.15:3.3 | S: wherever the perfected ones live | T: Wherever the arahants dwell
sn11.15:3.4 | S: is a delightful place.” | T: Is truly a delightful place.”

## sn11.16
sn11.16:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rajagaha on Mount Vulture Peak.
sn11.16:1.2 | S: And then Sakka, lord of gods, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and stood to one side.
sn11.16:1.3 | S: and addressed him in verse: | T: Standing to one side, he addressed the Blessed One in verse:
sn11.16:2.1 | S: “For humans, those merit-seeking creatures, | T: “For those people who bestow alms,
sn11.16:2.2 | S: who sponsor sacrifices, | T: For living beings in quest of merit,
sn11.16:2.3 | S: making worldly merit, | T: Performing merit of the mundane type,
sn11.16:2.4 | S: where is a gift very fruitful?” | T: Where does a gift bear great fruit?”
sn11.16:3.1 | S: “Four practicing the path, | T: The Blessed One: “The four practising the way
sn11.16:3.2 | S: and four established in the fruit. | T: And the four established in the fruit:
sn11.16:3.3 | S: This is the upright Saṅgha, | T: This is the Saṅgha of upright conduct
sn11.16:3.4 | S: with wisdom, ethics, and immersion. | T: Endowed with wisdom and virtue.
sn11.16:4.1 | S: For humans, those merit-seeking creatures, | T: “For those people who bestow alms,
sn11.16:4.2 | S: who sponsor sacrifices, | T: For living beings in quest of merit,
sn11.16:4.3 | S: making worldly merit, | T: Performing merit of the mundane type,
sn11.16:4.4 | S: what is given to the Saṅgha is very fruitful.” | T: A gift to the Saṅgha bears great fruit.”

## sn11.17
sn11.17:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: At Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove.
sn11.17:1.2 | S: Now at that time the Buddha had gone into retreat for the day’s meditation. | T: Now on that occasion the Blessed One had gone for his day’s abiding and was in seclusion.
sn11.17:1.3 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, and the divinity Sahampati approached the Buddha and stationed themselves one by each door-post. | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, and Brahma Sahampati approached the Blessed One and stood one at each doorpost.
sn11.17:1.4 | S: Then Sakka spoke this verse in the Buddha’s presence: | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, recited this verse in the presence of the Blessed One:
sn11.17:2.1 | S: “Rise, hero! Victor in battle, with burden put down, | T: “Rise up, O hero, victor in battle! Your burden lowered,
sn11.17:2.2 | S: wander the world free of debt. | T: debt-free one, wander in the world.
sn11.17:2.3 | S: Your mind is fully liberated, | T: Your mind is fully liberated
sn11.17:2.4 | S: like the moon on the fifteenth night.” | T: Like the moon on the fifteenth night.”
sn11.17:3.1 | S: “Lord of gods, that’s not how to pay homage to the Realized Ones. | T: Brahma Sahampati: “It is not in such a way that the Tathagatas are to be venerated, lord of the devas.
sn11.17:3.2 | S: This is how it should be done: | T: The Tathagatas are to be venerated thus:
sn11.17:4.1 | S: ‘Rise, hero! Victor in battle, caravan leader, | T: “Rise up, O hero, victor in battle! O caravan leader,
sn11.17:4.2 | S: wander the world free of debt. | T: debt-free one, wander in the world.
sn11.17:4.3 | S: Let the Blessed One teach the Dhamma! | T: Teach the Dhamma, O Blessed One:
sn11.17:4.4 | S: There will be those who understand!’” | T: There will be those who will understand.”

## sn11.21
sn11.21:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: At Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove.
sn11.21:1.2 | S: And then Sakka, lord of gods, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and stood to one side.
sn11.21:1.3 | S: and addressed him in verse: | T: Standing to one side, Sakka, lord of the devas, addressed the Blessed One in verse:
sn11.21:2.1 | S: “When what is slain do you sleep at ease? | T: “Having slain what does one sleep soundly?
sn11.21:2.2 | S: When what is slain is there no sorrow? | T: Having slain what does one not sorrow?
sn11.21:2.3 | S: What is the one thing | T: What is the one thing,
sn11.21:2.4 | S: whose killing you approve?” | T: O Gotama, Whose killing you approve?”
sn11.21:3.1 | S: “When anger’s slain you sleep at ease. | T: The Blessed One: “Having slain anger, one sleeps soundly;
sn11.21:3.2 | S: When anger’s slain there is no sorrow. | T: Having slain anger, one does not sorrow;
sn11.21:3.3 | S: Anger has a poisonous root | T: The killing of anger,
sn11.21:3.4 | S: and a sweet tip, O Vāsava. | T: O Vasava, With its poisoned root and honeyed tip:
sn11.21:3.5 | S: The noble ones praise its killing, | T: This is the killing the noble ones praise,
sn11.21:3.6 | S: for when it’s slain there is no sorrow.” | T: For having slain that, one does not sorrow.”

## sn11.22
sn11.22:1.1 | S: Near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. | T: At Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove.
sn11.22:1.2 | P: Tatra kho …pe… etadavoca: | T: There the Blessed One said this:
sn11.22:1.3 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, there was a native spirit who was ugly and deformed. He sat on the throne of Sakka, lord of gods. | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past a certain ugly deformed yakkha sat down on the seat of Sakka, lord of the devas.
sn11.22:1.4 | S: But the gods of the thirty-three complained, grumbled, and objected, | T: Thereupon the Tavatiṁsa devas found fault with this, grumbled, and complained about it, saying:
sn11.22:1.5 | S: ‘Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: ‘It is wonderful indeed, sir! It is amazing indeed, sir!
sn11.22:1.6 | S: This ugly and deformed spirit is sitting on the throne of Sakka, the lord of gods.’ | T: This ugly deformed yakkha has sat down on the seat of Sakka, lord of the devas!’
sn11.22:1.7 | S: But the more the gods complained, the more attractive, good-looking, and lovely that spirit became. | T: But to whatever extent the Tavatiṁsa devas found fault with this, grumbled, and complained about it, to the same extent that yakkha became more and more handsome, more and more comely, more and more graceful.
sn11.22:2.1 | S: So the gods went up to Sakka and told him what had happened. He replied, | T: “Then, bhikkhus, the Tavatiṁsa devas approached Sakka and said to him:
sn11.22:2.2 | P: ‘idha te, mārisa, aññataro yakkho dubbaṇṇo okoṭimako sakkassa devānamindassa āsane nisinno. | T: ‘Here, dear sir, an ugly deformed yakkha has sat down on your seat….
sn11.22:2.3 | P: Tatra sudaṁ, mārisa, devā tāvatiṁsā ujjhāyanti khiyyanti vipācenti: | T: (none)
sn11.22:2.4 | P: “acchariyaṁ vata bho, abbhutaṁ vata bho. | T: (none)
sn11.22:2.5 | P: Ayaṁ yakkho dubbaṇṇo okoṭimako sakkassa devānamindassa āsane nisinno”ti. | T: (none)
sn11.22:2.6 | P: Yathā yathā kho, mārisa, devā ujjhāyanti khiyyanti vipācenti tathā tathā so yakkho abhirūpataro ceva hoti dassanīyataro ca pāsādikataro cāti. | T: But to whatever extent the devas found fault with this … that yakkha became more and more handsome, more and more comely, more and more graceful.’—
sn11.22:2.7 | S: ‘Surely, good fellow, that must be the anger-eating spirit!’ | T: ‘That must be the anger-eating yakkha.’
sn11.22:3.1 | S: Then Sakka went up to that spirit, arranged his robe over one shoulder, knelt on his right knee, raised his joined palms toward the anger-eating spirit, and pronounced his name three times: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, Sakka, lord of the devas, approached that anger-eating yakkha. Having approached, he arranged his upper robe over one shoulder, knelt down with his right knee on the ground, and, raising his joined hands in reverential salutation towards that yakkha, he announced his name three times:
sn11.22:3.2 | S: ‘Good fellow, I am Sakka, lord of gods! Good fellow, I am Sakka, the lord of gods!’ | T: ‘I, dear sir, am Sakka, lord of the devas! I, dear sir, am Sakka, lord of the devas!’
sn11.22:3.3 | S: But the more Sakka pronounced his name, the uglier and more deformed the spirit became, | T: To whatever extent Sakka announced his name, to the same extent that yakkha became uglier and uglier and more and more deformed
sn11.22:3.4 | S: until eventually it vanished right there. | T: until he disappeared right there.
sn11.22:3.5 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, guiding the gods of the thirty-three, spoke this verse: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, having sat down on his own seat, instructing the Tavatiṁsa devas, Sakka, lord of the devas, on that occasion recited these verses:
sn11.22:4.1 | S: ‘My mind isn’t easily upset; | T: “‘I am not one afflicted in mind,
sn11.22:4.2 | S: I’m not easily drawn into the maelstrom. | T: Nor easily drawn by anger’s whirl.
sn11.22:4.3 | S: I don’t get angry for long, | T: I never become angry for long,
sn11.22:4.4 | S: anger doesn’t last in me. | T: Nor does anger persist in me.
sn11.22:5.1 | S: When I do get angry I don’t speak harshly, | T: “‘When I’m angry I don’t speak harshly
sn11.22:5.2 | S: nor do I advertise my own virtues. | T: And I don’t praise my virtues.
sn11.22:5.3 | S: I carefully restrain myself | T: I keep myself well restrained
sn11.22:5.4 | S: out of regard for my own welfare.’” | T: Out of regard for my own good.’”

## sn11.9
sn11.9:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn11.9:1.2 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, several seers who were ethical, of good character, settled in leaf huts in a wilderness region. | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past a number of seers who were virtuous and of good character had settled down in leaf huts in a tract of forest.
sn11.9:1.3 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, and Vepacitti, lord of titans, went to those seers. | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, and Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, approached those seers.
sn11.9:1.4 | S: Vepacitti put on his lined sandals, strapped on his sword, and, carrying a sunshade, entered the hermitage through the main gate. He walked right past those seers, keeping them at arm’s length. | T: “Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, put on his boots, bound his sword on tightly, and, with a parasol borne aloft, entered the hermitage through the main gate; then, having turned his left side towards them, he walked past those seers who were virtuous and of good character.
sn11.9:1.5 | S: Sakka took off his lined sandals, gave his sword to others, and, putting down his sunshade, entered the hermitage through the same gate. He stood downwind of those seers, revering them with cupped palms. | T: But Sakka, lord of the devas, took off his boots, handed over his sword to others, lowered his parasol, and entered the hermitage through an ordinary gate; then he stood on the lee side, raising his joined hands in reverential salutation, paying homage to those seers who were virtuous and of good character.
sn11.9:1.6 | S: Then those seers addressed Sakka in verse: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, those seers addressed Sakka in verse:
sn11.9:2.1 | S: ‘When seers have been long initiated, | T: “‘The odour of the seers long bound by their vows,
sn11.9:2.2 | S: the odor of their bodies goes with the gale. | T: Emitted from their bodies, goes with the wind.
sn11.9:2.3 | S: You’d better leave, O thousand-eyed! | T: Turn away from here, O thousand-eyed god,
sn11.9:2.4 | S: The odor of the seers is unclean, king of gods.’ | T: For the seers’ odour is foul, O deva-king.’
sn11.9:3.1 | S: ‘When seers have been long initiated, | T: Sakka: “‘Let the odour of the seers long bound by their vows,
sn11.9:3.2 | S: let the odor of their bodies go with the gale. | T: Emitted from their bodies, go with the wind;
sn11.9:3.3 | S: We yearn for this odor, sirs, | T: We yearn for this odour, O venerable sirs,
sn11.9:3.4 | S: like a colorful crown of flowers. | T: As for a garland of flowers on the head.
sn11.9:3.5 | S: The gods don’t see it as repulsive.’” | T: The devas do not perceive it as repulsive.’”

## sn12.11
sn12.11:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn12.11:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. … | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park….
sn12.11:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, these four fuels maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.11:1.4 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn12.11:1.5 | S: Edible food, whether solid or subtle; contact is the second, mental intention the third, and consciousness the fourth. | T: The nutriment edible food, gross or subtle; second, contact; third, mental volition; fourth, consciousness.
sn12.11:1.6 | S: These are the four fuels that maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: These are the four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.11:2.1 | S: What is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of these four fuels? | T: “Bhikkhus, these four kinds of nutriment have what as their source, what as their origin, from what are they born and produced?
sn12.11:2.2 | S: Craving. | T: These four kinds of nutriment have craving as their source, craving as their origin; they are born and produced from craving.
sn12.11:2.3 | S: And what is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of craving? | T: “And this craving has what as its source, what as its origin, from what is it born and produced?
sn12.11:2.4 | S: Feeling. | T: This craving has feeling as its source, feeling as its origin; it is born and produced from feeling.
sn12.11:2.5 | S: And what is the source of feeling? | T: “And this feeling has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.6 | S: Contact. | T: Feeling has contact as its source….
sn12.11:2.7 | S: And what is the source of contact? | T: And this contact has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.8 | S: The six sense fields. | T: Contact has the six sense bases as its source….
sn12.11:2.9 | S: And what is the source of the six sense fields? | T: And these six sense bases have what as their source…?
sn12.11:2.10 | S: Name and form. | T: The six sense bases have name-and-form as their source….
sn12.11:2.11 | S: And what is the source of name and form? | T: And this name-and-form has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.12 | S: Consciousness. | T: Name-and-form has consciousness as its source….
sn12.11:2.13 | S: And what is the source of consciousness? | T: And this consciousness has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.14 | S: Choices. | T: Consciousness has volitional formations as its source….
sn12.11:2.15 | S: And what is the source of choices? | T: And these volitional formations have what as their source, what as their origin, from what are they born and produced?
sn12.11:2.16 | S: Ignorance. | T: Volitional formations have ignorance as their source, ignorance as their origin; they are born and produced from ignorance.
sn12.11:3.1 | S: And so, mendicants, ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “Thus, bhikkhus, with ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.11:3.2 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.11:3.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.11:3.4 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: But with the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance comes cessation of volitional formations; with the cessation of volitional formations,
sn12.11:3.5 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: cessation of consciousness….
sn12.11:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.11:3.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.15
sn12.15:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.15:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Kaccānagotta went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Kaccanagotta approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn12.15:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘right view’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘right view, right view.’
sn12.15:1.4 | S: How is right view defined?” | T: In what way, venerable sir, is there right view?”
sn12.15:2.1 | S: “Kaccāna, this world mostly relies on the duality of existence and non-existence. | T: “This world, Kaccana, for the most part depends upon a duality—
sn12.15:2.2 | S: But for one who truly sees the origin of the world with right understanding, the concept of non-existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: upon the notion of existence and the notion of nonexistence. But for one who sees the origin of the world as it really is with correct wisdom, there is no notion of nonexistence in regard to the world.
sn12.15:2.3 | S: And for one who truly sees the cessation of the world with right understanding, the concept of existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: And for one who sees the cessation of the world as it really is with correct wisdom, there is no notion of existence in regard to the world.
sn12.15:2.4 | S: This world is for the most part shackled by getting involved, grasping, and insisting. | T: “This world, Kaccana, is for the most part shackled by engagement, clinging, and adherence.
sn12.15:2.5 | S: But if, when it comes to this getting involved & grasping, mental fixation, and insistence & underlying tendency, they are not attracted, do not grasp, and do not fixate on the thought, “my self”, | T: But this one with right view does not become engaged and cling through that engagement and clinging, mental standpoint, adherence, underlying tendency; he does not take a stand about ‘my self.’
sn12.15:2.6 | S: they’ll have no doubt or uncertainty that what arises is just suffering arising, and what ceases is just suffering ceasing. Their knowledge of this is independent of others. | T: He has no perplexity or doubt that what arises is only suffering arising, what ceases is only suffering ceasing. His knowledge about this is independent of others.
sn12.15:2.7 | S: This is how right view is defined. | T: It is in this way, Kaccana, that there is right view.
sn12.15:3.1 | S: ‘All exists’: this is one extreme. | T: “‘All exists’: Kaccana, this is one extreme.
sn12.15:3.2 | S: ‘All does not exist’: this is the second extreme. | T: ‘All does not exist’: this is the second extreme.
sn12.15:3.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.15:3.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: ‘With ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.15:3.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.15:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.15:3.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: But with the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance comes cessation of volitional formations; with the cessation of volitional formations,
sn12.15:3.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: cessation of consciousness….
sn12.15:3.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.15:3.10 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.18
sn12.18:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.18:1.2 | S: Then the wanderer Timbaruka went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the wanderer Timbaruka approached the Blessed One and exchanged greetings with him.
sn12.18:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to him:
sn12.18:2.1 | S: “Well, worthy Gotama, are pleasure and pain made by oneself?” | T: “How is it, Master Gotama: are pleasure and pain created by oneself?”
sn12.18:2.2 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Timbaruka,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.3 | S: “Then are pleasure and pain made by another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, are pleasure and pain created by another?”
sn12.18:2.4 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Timbaruka,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.5 | S: “Well, are pleasure and pain made by both oneself and another?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: are pleasure and pain created both by oneself and by another?”
sn12.18:2.6 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Timbaruka,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.7 | S: “Then do pleasure and pain arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “Then, Master Gotama, have pleasure and pain arisen fortuitously, being created neither by oneself nor by another?”
sn12.18:2.8 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Not so, Timbaruka,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.9 | S: “Well, is there no such thing as pleasure and pain?” | T: “How is it then, Master Gotama: is there no pleasure and pain?”
sn12.18:2.10 | S: “It’s not that there’s no such thing as pleasure and pain. | T: “It is not that there is no pleasure and pain, Timbaruka;
sn12.18:2.11 | S: Pleasure and pain are real.” | T: there is pleasure and pain.”
sn12.18:2.12 | S: “Then does the worthy Gotama not know or see suffering?” | T: “Then is it that Master Gotama does not know and see pleasure and pain?”
sn12.18:2.13 | S: “It’s not that I don’t know or see pleasure and pain. | T: “It is not that I do not know and see pleasure and pain, Timbaruka.
sn12.18:2.14 | S: I do know pleasure and pain, | T: I know pleasure and pain,
sn12.18:2.15 | S: I do see pleasure and pain.” | T: I see pleasure and pain.”

