## sn12.19
sn12.19:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.19:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, for a fool shrouded by ignorance and coupled to craving, this body has been produced. | T: “Bhikkhus, for the fool, hindered by ignorance and fettered by craving, this body has thereby originated.
sn12.19:1.3 | S: So there is this duality: this body and external name and form. Contact depends on this duality. When contacted through one or other of the six sense fields, the fool experiences pleasure and pain. | T: So there is this body and external name-and-form: thus this dyad. Dependent on the dyad there is contact. There are just six sense bases, contacted through which—or through a certain one among them—the fool experiences pleasure and pain.
sn12.19:2.1 | S: For an astute person shrouded by ignorance and coupled to craving, this body has been produced. | T: “Bhikkhus, for the wise man, hindered by ignorance and fettered by craving, this body has thereby originated.
sn12.19:2.2 | S: So there is this duality: this body and external name and form. Contact depends on this duality. When contacted through one or other of the six sense fields, the astute person experiences pleasure and pain. | T: So there is this body and external name-and-form: thus this dyad. Dependent on the dyad there is contact. There are just six sense bases, contacted through which—or through a certain one among them—the wise man experiences pleasure and pain.
sn12.19:3.1 | S: What, then, is the difference between the foolish and the astute?” | T: What, bhikkhus, is the distinction here, what is the disparity, what is the difference between the wise man and the fool?”
sn12.19:3.2 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “Venerable sir, our teachings are rooted in the Blessed One, guided by the Blessed One, take recourse in the Blessed One. It would be good if the Blessed One would clear up the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from him, the bhikkhus will remember it.”
sn12.19:4.1 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then listen and attend closely, bhikkhus, I will speak.”
sn12.19:4.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” the bhikkhus replied.
sn12.19:4.3 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn12.19:5.1 | S: “For a fool shrouded by ignorance and coupled to craving, this body has been produced. But the fool has not given up that ignorance or finished that craving. | T: “Bhikkhus, for the fool, hindered by ignorance and fettered by craving, this body has originated. For the fool that ignorance has not been abandoned and that craving has not been utterly destroyed.
sn12.19:5.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn12.19:5.3 | S: The fool has not completed the spiritual journey for the complete ending of suffering. | T: Because the fool has not lived the holy life for the complete destruction of suffering.
sn12.19:5.4 | S: Therefore, when their body breaks up, the fool passes on to another body. | T: Therefore, with the breakup of the body, the fool fares on to another body.
sn12.19:5.5 | S: When they have passed on to another body, they’re not freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: Faring on to another body, he is not freed from birth, aging, and death; not freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair;
sn12.19:5.6 | S: They’re not freed from suffering, I say. | T: not freed from suffering, I say.
sn12.19:6.1 | S: For an astute person shrouded by ignorance and coupled to craving, this body has been produced. But the astute person has given up that ignorance and finished that craving. | T: “Bhikkhus, for the wise man, hindered by ignorance and fettered by craving, this body has originated. For the wise man that ignorance has been abandoned and that craving has been utterly destroyed.
sn12.19:6.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn12.19:6.3 | S: The astute person has completed the spiritual journey for the complete ending of suffering. | T: Because the wise man has lived the holy life for the complete destruction of suffering.
sn12.19:6.4 | S: Therefore, when their body breaks up, the astute person does not pass on to another body. | T: Therefore, with the breakup of the body, the wise man does not fare on to another body.
sn12.19:6.5 | S: Not passing on to another body, they’re freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: Not faring on to another body, he is freed from birth, aging, and death; freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair;
sn12.19:6.6 | S: They’re freed from suffering, I say. | T: freed from suffering, I say.
sn12.19:6.7 | S: This is the difference here between the foolish and the astute, that is, leading the spiritual life.” | T: “This, bhikkhus, is the distinction, the disparity, the difference between the wise man and the fool, that is, the living of the holy life.”
sn12.19:6.8 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


