## sn12.11
sn12.11:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn12.11:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. … | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park….
sn12.11:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, these four fuels maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.11:1.4 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn12.11:1.5 | S: Edible food, whether solid or subtle; contact is the second, mental intention the third, and consciousness the fourth. | T: The nutriment edible food, gross or subtle; second, contact; third, mental volition; fourth, consciousness.
sn12.11:1.6 | S: These are the four fuels that maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: These are the four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.11:2.1 | S: What is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of these four fuels? | T: “Bhikkhus, these four kinds of nutriment have what as their source, what as their origin, from what are they born and produced?
sn12.11:2.2 | S: Craving. | T: These four kinds of nutriment have craving as their source, craving as their origin; they are born and produced from craving.
sn12.11:2.3 | S: And what is the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of craving? | T: “And this craving has what as its source, what as its origin, from what is it born and produced?
sn12.11:2.4 | S: Feeling. | T: This craving has feeling as its source, feeling as its origin; it is born and produced from feeling.
sn12.11:2.5 | S: And what is the source of feeling? | T: “And this feeling has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.6 | S: Contact. | T: Feeling has contact as its source….
sn12.11:2.7 | S: And what is the source of contact? | T: And this contact has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.8 | S: The six sense fields. | T: Contact has the six sense bases as its source….
sn12.11:2.9 | S: And what is the source of the six sense fields? | T: And these six sense bases have what as their source…?
sn12.11:2.10 | S: Name and form. | T: The six sense bases have name-and-form as their source….
sn12.11:2.11 | S: And what is the source of name and form? | T: And this name-and-form has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.12 | S: Consciousness. | T: Name-and-form has consciousness as its source….
sn12.11:2.13 | S: And what is the source of consciousness? | T: And this consciousness has what as its source…?
sn12.11:2.14 | S: Choices. | T: Consciousness has volitional formations as its source….
sn12.11:2.15 | S: And what is the source of choices? | T: And these volitional formations have what as their source, what as their origin, from what are they born and produced?
sn12.11:2.16 | S: Ignorance. | T: Volitional formations have ignorance as their source, ignorance as their origin; they are born and produced from ignorance.
sn12.11:3.1 | S: And so, mendicants, ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “Thus, bhikkhus, with ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.11:3.2 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.11:3.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.11:3.4 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: But with the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance comes cessation of volitional formations; with the cessation of volitional formations,
sn12.11:3.5 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: cessation of consciousness….
sn12.11:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.11:3.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.15
sn12.15:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.15:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Kaccānagotta went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Kaccanagotta approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn12.15:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘right view’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘right view, right view.’
sn12.15:1.4 | S: How is right view defined?” | T: In what way, venerable sir, is there right view?”
sn12.15:2.1 | S: “Kaccāna, this world mostly relies on the duality of existence and non-existence. | T: “This world, Kaccana, for the most part depends upon a duality—
sn12.15:2.2 | S: But for one who truly sees the origin of the world with right understanding, the concept of non-existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: upon the notion of existence and the notion of nonexistence. But for one who sees the origin of the world as it really is with correct wisdom, there is no notion of nonexistence in regard to the world.
sn12.15:2.3 | S: And for one who truly sees the cessation of the world with right understanding, the concept of existence regarding the world does not occur. | T: And for one who sees the cessation of the world as it really is with correct wisdom, there is no notion of existence in regard to the world.
sn12.15:2.4 | S: This world is for the most part shackled by getting involved, grasping, and insisting. | T: “This world, Kaccana, is for the most part shackled by engagement, clinging, and adherence.
sn12.15:2.5 | S: But if, when it comes to this getting involved & grasping, mental fixation, and insistence & underlying tendency, they are not attracted, do not grasp, and do not fixate on the thought, “my self”, | T: But this one with right view does not become engaged and cling through that engagement and clinging, mental standpoint, adherence, underlying tendency; he does not take a stand about ‘my self.’
sn12.15:2.6 | S: they’ll have no doubt or uncertainty that what arises is just suffering arising, and what ceases is just suffering ceasing. Their knowledge of this is independent of others. | T: He has no perplexity or doubt that what arises is only suffering arising, what ceases is only suffering ceasing. His knowledge about this is independent of others.
sn12.15:2.7 | S: This is how right view is defined. | T: It is in this way, Kaccana, that there is right view.
sn12.15:3.1 | S: ‘All exists’: this is one extreme. | T: “‘All exists’: Kaccana, this is one extreme.
sn12.15:3.2 | S: ‘All does not exist’: this is the second extreme. | T: ‘All does not exist’: this is the second extreme.
sn12.15:3.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma by the middle:
sn12.15:3.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: ‘With ignorance as condition, volitional formations come to be;
sn12.15:3.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: with volitional formations as condition, consciousness….
sn12.15:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.15:3.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: But with the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance comes cessation of volitional formations; with the cessation of volitional formations,
sn12.15:3.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: cessation of consciousness….
sn12.15:3.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.15:3.10 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

